⍝ lib/prove.imo — spec notation — trusted prelude, part 12: the predicates on ℝ[i] — relations decided by logic (true, false, undetermined)
⍝ An element of ℝ[i] is defined logically (by its definition: a series, a sum, a product, …), so a relation between two of them is
⍝ decided from the definitions, not from the streams.  The prover receives the relation φ and every definition it uses as CODES — terms
⍝ written with numbers, a symbol by its doc 1 code — and decides φ by
⍝   logical expansion   unfold definitions (β-reduction on codes);
⍝   simplification      the ring normal form over atoms (series, exponentials, opaque terms), as for algebraic expressions;
⍝   isomorphism         two series are the same element when their first terms and their ratios k ↦ ρ(k) agree (by induction on k);
⍝   proof rules         Σ (k ↦ c/k) is exp c;  the sine / cosine / hyperbolic series are exponentials;  exp a · exp b = exp (a + b);
⍝   certified bounds    a convergent series lies in a disc around its partial sum (ratio test), which refutes = and decides < .
⍝ The decision is ⋗ ≖ ⋖ in 𝔒 (⋖ false < ≖ not decided < ⋗ true): "and" is min, "or" is max, "not" reverses.
⍝ Statements (docs/predicates.md): every statement — a relation on ℝ[i], a relation or predicate about an UNKNOWN (a symbol declared
⍝ and never defined), a connective on statements — has class Ω (Prop) and is simplified to its simplest form whenever it is evaluated
⍝ or output: the compiler writes it as (simplify′ ε⌜⌝ ⌜φ⌝), and simplify′ (lib/solve.imo) returns the code of true, of false, or of the
⍝ simplest equivalent statement.  A predicate is a function whose value is true, false or undetermined.
⍝ As a truth value (decide, Ω → 𝔹) it is ⊤ or ⊥ (the digits 1 and 0), or ⍰ (undetermined) when it is neither — never a guess.
⍝ ---- codes
𝔔 ∶ 𝒦
⍝ ---- statements (Prop): the class of statements in simplest form.  A value of Ω IS a code (𝔔) — the code of the simplest statement
⍝ the simplifier found (lib/solve.imo, simplify′): (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊤) is true, (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊥) is false, anything else a statement that is neither.
Ω ∶ 𝒦   ⍝ statements in simplest form (Prop); a value is the code of the statement
𝔮ⁿ ∶ α → 𝔔
𝔮ˢ ∶ 𝔇 → 𝔔
𝔮ᵃ ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔔 → 𝔔
𝔮ˡ ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔔
𝔮ᵛ ∶ 𝔇 → 𝔔
𝔮ⁿ ξ ≔ (cn cs ca cl cv ↦ (cn ξ))
𝔮ˢ ξ ≔ (cn cs ca cl cv ↦ (cs ξ))
𝔮ᵃ ξ η ≔ (cn cs ca cl cv ↦ (ca ξ η))
𝔮ˡ ξ ≔ (cn cs ca cl cv ↦ (cl ξ))
𝔮ᵛ ξ ≔ (cn cs ca cl cv ↦ (cv ξ))
⍝ undetermined: a Boolean-like value that absorbs its arguments (an "if" on it is undetermined too); the host reports it, never as true or false
⍰ ∶ 𝔹
⍰ ≔ (ξ η ↦ ⍰)
⍝ ---- three-valued logic on 𝔒: or = max, and = min, not = reverse
∨³ ∶ 𝔒 → 𝔒 → 𝔒
∨³ ξ η ≔ (ξ η (η ≖ ≖ ⋗) ⋗)
∧³ ∶ 𝔒 → 𝔒 → 𝔒
∧³ ξ η ≔ (ξ ⋖ (η ⋖ ≖ ≖) η)
¬³ ∶ 𝔒 → 𝔒
¬³ ξ ≔ (ξ ⋗ ≖ ⋖)
⍝ ---- one system.  ⊤ and ⊥ ARE the digits 1 and 0, so a truth value is a number: ι (the Iverson bracket) widens 𝔹 to ℕ, and supp
⍝ (the support, n ≠ 0) maps back.  ι turns "or" into max and "and" into min (= ×); supp turns + into "or" and × into "and"; the union of
⍝ two sets is "or" of their membership tests.  The human notation therefore spells all of them with the same symbols (+ max or union …).
ι ∶ 𝔹 → ℕ
ι ξ ≔ (ξ 1 0)
(↪ ι)
supp ∶ ℕ → 𝔹
supp ξ ≔ (¬ (≐ ξ 0))
⍝ ---- value equality of Gaussian integers (for indices and codes)
=ᶻ ∶ 𝔇 → 𝔇 → 𝔹
=ᶻ ξ η ≔ (∅ᶻ (⊟ᶻ ξ η))
⍝ ---- the head and the arguments of a code
spine′ ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔏 𝔔 → 𝔓 𝔔 (𝔏 𝔔)
spine′ ξ λ ≔ (ξ (θ ↦ (⊗ ξ λ)) (θ ↦ (⊗ ξ λ)) (φ ψ ↦ (spine′ φ (∷ ψ λ))) (θ ↦ (⊗ ξ λ)) (θ ↦ (⊗ ξ λ)))
spine ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔓 𝔔 (𝔏 𝔔)
spine ξ ≔ (spine′ ξ ⊥)
⍝ the code of a symbol, if the code is a symbol (else the code −1, which no symbol has)
symᶜ ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔇
symᶜ ξ ≔ (ξ (θ ↦ (◀ 11101)) (θ ↦ θ) (φ ψ ↦ (◀ 11101)) (θ ↦ (◀ 11101)) (θ ↦ (◀ 11101)))
apps ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔏 𝔔 → 𝔔
apps ξ λ ≔ (foldl 𝔮ᵃ ξ λ)
⍝ ---- de Bruijn shifting and substitution on codes, β-reduction
⇑ᶜ ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔇 → 𝔇 → 𝔔
⇑ᶜ ξ δ ϑ ≔ (ξ (θ ↦ ξ) (θ ↦ ξ) (φ ψ ↦ (𝔮ᵃ (⇑ᶜ φ δ ϑ) (⇑ᶜ ψ δ ϑ))) (θ ↦ (𝔮ˡ (⇑ᶜ θ δ (⊞ᶻ ϑ (◀ 1))))) (θ ↦ ((⩾ᶻ θ ϑ) (𝔮ᵛ (⊞ᶻ θ δ)) ξ)))
⇓ᶜ ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔇 → 𝔔 → 𝔔
⇓ᶜ ξ ϑ ω ≔ (ξ (θ ↦ ξ) (θ ↦ ξ) (φ ψ ↦ (𝔮ᵃ (⇓ᶜ φ ϑ ω) (⇓ᶜ ψ ϑ ω))) (θ ↦ (𝔮ˡ (⇓ᶜ θ (⊞ᶻ ϑ (◀ 1)) (⇑ᶜ ω (◀ 1) (◀ 1))))) (θ ↦ ((=ᶻ θ ϑ) ω ((⩾ᶻ θ ϑ) (𝔮ᵛ (⊟ᶻ θ (◀ 1))) ξ))))
βᶜ ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔔 → 𝔔
βᶜ ξ ω ≔ (⇓ᶜ ξ (◀ 1) ω)
⍝ apply a code to a list of argument codes, β-reducing while the code is a λ
βs ∶ 𝔔 → 𝔏 𝔔 → 𝔔
βs ξ λ ≔ (λ (θ ι ↦ (ξ (φ ↦ (apps ξ λ)) (φ ↦ (apps ξ λ)) (φ ψ ↦ (apps ξ λ)) (φ ↦ (βs (βᶜ φ θ) ι)) (φ ↦ (apps ξ λ)))) ξ)
⍝ ---- definitions: ε is a list of (code of a symbol, code of its definition)
look ∶ 𝔏 (𝔓 𝔇 𝔔) → 𝔇 → 𝔓 𝔹 𝔔
look ε ξ ≔ (ε (θ ι ↦ ((=ᶻ (◀ θ) ξ) (⊗ ⊤ (▶ θ)) (look ι ξ))) (⊗ ⊥ ⊥))
⍝ one step of logical expansion: (f a b …) with f defined becomes the definition applied to a b …, and ((λ …) a …) is β-reduced;
⍝ (⊤, code) or (⊥, _)
unfold ∶ 𝔏 (𝔓 𝔇 𝔔) → 𝔔 → 𝔓 𝔹 𝔔
unfold ε ξ ≔ ((σ ↦ ((υ ↦ ((◀ σ) (θ ↦ υ) (θ ↦ υ) (φ ψ ↦ υ) (θ ↦ (⊗ ⊤ (βs (◀ σ) (▶ σ)))) (θ ↦ υ))) ((τ ↦ ((◀ τ) (⊗ ⊤ (βs (▶ τ) (▶ σ))) (⊗ ⊥ ⊥))) (look ε (symᶜ (◀ σ)))))) (spine ξ))
⍝ ---- the predicates on ℝ[i].  The compiler (pymo/imotypes.py, pymo/quote.py; haskimo/src/I1/Types.hs, Quote.hs) replaces every statement —
⍝ a relation on ℝ[i], a relation whose sides mention an unknown, a connective with a statement operand — by  (simplify′ ⌜definitions⌝ ⌜φ⌝),
⍝ so these bodies are only reached when a relation is used as a value without arguments.
≐ᴿ ∶ ℝ[i] → ℝ[i] → Ω
≐ᴿ ξ η ≔ ⍰
≠ᴿ ∶ ℝ[i] → ℝ[i] → Ω
≠ᴿ ξ η ≔ ⍰
≺ᴿ ∶ ℝ → ℝ → Ω
≺ᴿ ξ η ≔ ⍰
⩽ᴿ ∶ ℝ → ℝ → Ω
⩽ᴿ ξ η ≔ ⍰
≻ᴿ ∶ ℝ → ℝ → Ω
≻ᴿ ξ η ≔ ⍰
⩾ᴿ ∶ ℝ → ℝ → Ω
⩾ᴿ ξ η ≔ ⍰
≠ ∶ ℚ[i] → ℚ[i] → 𝔹
≠ ξ η ≔ (¬ (≐ ξ η))
⍝ ---- Prop and Bool.  prop and decide are the canonical coercions 𝔹 → Ω and Ω → 𝔹; decide is the lossy one (a statement that is
⍝ neither true nor false becomes ⍰), so the class checker counts it twice and prefers a reading in Ω when both fit.
truth_rd ∶ Ω → 𝔒   ⍝ the truth of a statement in simplest form: ⋗ true, ⋖ false, ≖ neither (helper)
truth_rd ξ ≔ (ξ (θ ↦ (θ ⋗ ⋖)) (θ ↦ ((=ᶻ θ ᶜ⊤) ⋗ ((=ᶻ θ ᶜ⊥) ⋖ ≖))) (φ ψ ↦ ≖) (θ ↦ ≖) (θ ↦ ≖))
prop ∶ 𝔹 → Ω   ⍝ a truth value as a statement: ⊤ is the statement true, ⊥ the statement false (canonical coercion 𝔹 → Ω)
prop ξ ≔ (ξ (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊤) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊥))
(↪ prop)
decide ∶ Ω → 𝔹   ⍝ a statement as a truth value: ⊤ if it is true, ⊥ if it is false, ⍰ (undetermined) otherwise (canonical coercion Ω → 𝔹)
decide ξ ≔ ((truth_rd ξ) ⊥ ⍰ ⊤)
(↪ decide)
⍝ the connectives on statements.  The compiler quotes every combination with a statement operand, so that it is simplified as one
⍝ statement; these bodies (used only when a connective is passed as a value) build the code of the combination, true and false absorbed.
∧Ω ∶ Ω → Ω → Ω   ⍝ and of two statements (simplified together)
∧Ω ξ η ≔ ((truth_rd ξ) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊥) ((truth_rd η) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊥) (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ˢ ᶜ∧) ξ) η) ξ) η)
∨Ω ∶ Ω → Ω → Ω   ⍝ or of two statements (simplified together)
∨Ω ξ η ≔ ((truth_rd ξ) η ((truth_rd η) ξ (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ˢ ᶜ∨) ξ) η) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊤)) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊤))
¬Ω ∶ Ω → Ω   ⍝ negation of a statement (simplified)
¬Ω ξ ≔ ((truth_rd ξ) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊤) (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ˢ ᶜ¬) ξ) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊥))
⇒Ω ∶ Ω → Ω → Ω   ⍝ implication between statements (simplified together)
⇒Ω ξ η ≔ ((truth_rd ξ) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊤) ((truth_rd η) (¬Ω ξ) (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⇒) ξ) η) (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⊤)) η)
⇔Ω ∶ Ω → Ω → Ω   ⍝ equivalence of statements (simplified together)
⇔Ω ξ η ≔ ((truth_rd ξ) (¬Ω η) ((truth_rd η) (¬Ω ξ) (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ᵃ (𝔮ˢ ᶜ⇔) ξ) η) ξ) η)
⍝ a statement computed at run time inside a quoted statement: its value is already a code
code_rd ∶ Ω → 𝔔   ⍝ the code of a statement (a statement is its code; helper of the compiler)
code_rd ξ ≔ ξ
